Conclusion: margins are useful fixed fibers, but the mixed product sign is a separate quotient guardrail even in the smallest model.

Tournament Analysis on proof carriers
pairwise observable: retention of LRC predicate, margins, mixed Haar sign, fixed-margin switch, color resonance, Fejer compatibility, tope/cocircuit labels, quotient guardrail
score_hist={0: 1, 1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 1, 4: 1, 5: 1, 6: 1}
directed_3cycles=0
SCC_sizes=[1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]
hamiltonian_paths=1
canonical_path=labelled_haar_square_packet > fixed_margin_switch_cocircuit > mixed_haar_discrepancy > colored_resonance_congruence > tope_cocircuit_wall_label > row_column_margin_shadow > raw_component_count_K

Synthesis
HYP-2594 counted continuous components K. HYP-2595 showed only color-compatible resonances survive. The Haar product square explains why: row/column boundary data are margins, while the surviving discrepancy is the mixed product coefficient.
HYP-2986's tope/cocircuit packets are the geometric version of the same switch: an open tope is a positive cell, AP/GW are boundary atoms, and a true residual would have to survive every mixed-product switch without exposing a witness.
Tournament tiling is therefore not a metaphor bolted on afterward. The fixed Hamiltonian path chooses the row/column observer; the Haar product tile records the orientation sign that the row/column quotient would otherwise forget.
